{"ld": 2132, "paper": "SP826", "session": "132", "title": "An Act to Amend the Maine Emergency Medical Services Act of 1982 to Modify Penalties for Unauthorized Operation or Practice of Emergency Medical Services, to Direct Maine Emergency Medical Services to Evaluate Improvements to Maine's Trauma System Reporting and to Make Certain Technical Changes (TITLE CHANGE)", "sponsor_id": "s-talbot-ross-rachel", "cosponsor_ids": [], "committee_code": "CJPS", "committee_name": "Criminal Justice and Public Safety", "summary": {"text": "This bill requires emergency medical services educators and emergency medical services training centers to be licensed by the Emergency Medical Services' Board. Current law provides that a person who operates or practices without a license commits a Class E crime. The bill specifies that a person must intentionally, knowingly or recklessly operate or practice without a license to commit a Class E crime. The bill also provides for a civil violation for operating or practicing without a license. It requires rather than allows physicians and hospitals to report trauma information to the board and amends the definition of \"regional council\" to specify that a council is an entity rather than a business entity.", "official": true, "source": "Bill text, https://legislature.maine.gov/legis/bills/getPDF.asp?paper=SP0826&item=1&snum=132"}, "status": "enacted", "status_label": "Enacted", "final_disposition": {"label": "Enacted", "date": "2026-04-13", "governor_action": "Signed", "governor_action_date": "2026-04-13"}, "fiscal_status": "Fiscal Impact", "documents": [{"label": "Bill text (LD 2132)", "url": "https://legislature.maine.gov/legis/bills/getPDF.asp?paper=SP0826&item=1&snum=132", "item": 1}, {"label": "Amendment C-A (S-583)", "url": "https://legislature.maine.gov/legis/bills/getPDF.asp?paper=SP0826&item=2&snum=132", "item": 2}], "amendments": [{"id": "C-A (S-583)", "description": null, "sponsor": null, "status": "Adopted by House & Senate", "ur